    "point": "§FS-workspace.1.3",
    "authoredFrom": "Read from the case's input bytes: grund.toml (version 1, marker §, `strict = false`, `[id] format = \"{kind}-{slug}\"` with slug pattern `[a-z][a-z0-9-]*`, default kinds, `[scan] include = [\"docs\"]`), FS-pinned.md (its heading, body lines 2-8) and the FS index with its one linked entry. Line 3's unmarked `path/FS-pinned` is a path in prose, not a citation: what makes the `alias/ID` shape a qualified citation is the marker (§FS-workspace.1.3), and an unmarked `alias/ID` is text in every repository, never promoted to a qualified citation nor cut back to a bare one (AR-workspace.3.1, §FS-citations.requirements.whole-token), so it earns neither an unknown-project-alias error (§FS-check.3.8) nor an edge. Line 7's bare FS-pinned, a citation in this compatibility mode (§FS-citations.recognized), and its marked §FS-pinned both cite the declaration from inside its own body; the heading never cites the ID it declares (§FS-citations.recognized.title-lines), and the index entry is no use (§FS-check.4.1.2), so the two body citations keep FS-pinned used. The one finding is the site-less version caution (§FS-check.2.1.1). Fmt adds no marker without --marker (§FS-fmt.2.2); the cross-reference pass wraps line 7's marked self-citation to its own heading (§FS-fmt.6.2, §FS-fmt.6.2.1) and leaves the bare one, the unmarked path and the already-canonical index entry (§FS-fmt.6.3) as written. FS-404 is no ID under this slug pattern, so FS-absent is the absent control.",
